{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/glj28k","slideIndex":0,"text":"Completed work. Algorithm 1 splits the active forest at ceil(n/2), pairs corresponding trees from the two halves, and carries the unmatched tree unchanged. The displayed values use exact tree evaluation. They establish algorithm shape, not a measured floating-point error. A carried tree contains no new addition node, so roundedSum introduces no extra round call. General proofs establish a singleton final forest, multiset preservation including duplicates, exact real sum preservation, and depth at most Nat.clog 2 n. For each operation the supplied Model assumes round(z)=z*(1+delta), abs(delta)<=u. This is an abstract assumption, not a proved IEEE 754 implementation or runtime overflow check. Under L*u<1, the numerical theorem constructs a same-shape perturbed tree whose exact sum equals roundedSum. Its leaf errors satisfy gamma L u. DotProduct.lean separately adds one rounded multiplication per leaf and proves a gamma(depth+1) perturbation bound on product values. Sources: Research/Projects/BinaryTreeReduction/Algorithm.lean, Examples.lean, Reduction.lean, DotProduct.lean, Research/Common/FloatingPoint/Model.lean. Historical verification: artifacts/runs/2026-10-11-algorithm1-offset-reduction.md."}
