{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/bp0bs4","slideIndex":1,"text":"Explain the trust boundary. I controlled the specification, acceptance tests, decomposition, and review. The coding agent generated and repaired substantial parts of the Lean code. I do not claim to have manually written every tactic. A theorem statement appears before := by, and the proof follows it. Tactics construct proof terms for Lean's kernel to check, within the trusted Lean and library assumptions. Structural induction separates leaf and node cases. At a node, use the two subtree results and one new rounding factor. The gamma recurrence bounds the new perturbation. In the implementation, the general numerical theorem uses induction on a depth budget k, generalizing over the tree. Multiset preservation retains duplicates while allowing reordered leaves. Lean verifies the proposition supplied to it. I back-translate that proposition to detect extra assumptions or a weakened conclusion. The recorded verification count includes dependency build jobs and is not a count of newly proved theorems. The repository scan rejects project-defined axiom declarations, not the standard logical foundations used by Lean and Mathlib. Proposed process auditing would additionally check retrieval logs and escalation decisions. Sources: pipeline/README.md, pipeline/verify.sh, projects/binary-tree-reduction/theorem.md, artifacts/runs/2026-10-11-algorithm1-offset-reduction.md. Detailed code: Research/Common/FloatingPoint/Model.lean, Gamma.lean, Research/Projects/BinaryTreeReduction/Algorithm.lean, Reduction.lean."}
