{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/29msji","slideIndex":2,"text":"This is the proposed evaluation, not a completed comparison. Persistent improvement here occurs through external memory while model weights remain fixed. Use new proof tasks whose completed answers are absent from the snapshot, and fresh agent sessions. For paired experiments use matched distinct theorems, or run the same target independently with strict isolation and no cross-run leakage. Hold settings and repository state fixed, freeze the artifact snapshot, and assign conditions before attempts. Include unsuccessful runs. Record unavailable metrics as unknown. Count memory creation and reflection costs separately as well as execution cost, so retrieval does not receive free preparation. The current run record reports unknown historical wall time, tokens, and cost. Across multiple checkpoints evaluate held-out performance against cumulative learning cost. The Agent Plasticity paper reports model- and task-dependent improvement, partial transfer, and a difference between final capability and acquisition efficiency. Artifact reuse correlates with improvement but high reuse does not guarantee success. Its weights stay frozen. Later diagnostic interventions could force relevant artifacts into context or substitute human-curated lessons to test retrieval versus quality. These extensions remain proposals. Source: C:/Users/austi/Downloads/2610.08902v1.pdf, Agent Plasticity: Measuring Self-Improvement Through Experience, sections 2-4. Local protocol: AGENTS.md and pipeline/README.md."}
