{"kind":"notes","id":"nt/v1uplk","slideIndex":3,"text":"This infrastructure is proposed and has not been implemented by this slide update. One monorepo remains the source of code, experiment configuration, and final results. A trusted host-side script prepares an allowlisted workspace for each run. The agent receives required definitions and unfinished theorem statements, not completed target proofs. Do not copy the full .git directory or target-bearing .olean outputs. Dependency caches are acceptable only if they cannot expose the evaluated answer. The stateless container has no artifact mount. The retrieved container receives the frozen snapshot read-only. Both start fresh with separate session stores and identical pinned agent version, model, reasoning settings, Lean and Mathlib versions. The remote model can request tools, but all filesystem and shell execution for the evaluated agent must stay inside this environment. Running only lake build in Docker does not isolate an outside agent's other file tools. Do not mount the full repository, host home, or Docker socket. Restrict egress to required model services and prevent access to the public target solution. Browser, MCP, and other tools must not bypass this boundary. Test isolation with sentinel files outside the permitted workspace before running the experiment. A trusted evaluator then runs its fixed verification command on exported output and performs semantic review. Preserve transcripts and available measurements. Folder boundaries and worktrees alone do not restrict reading. The two containers shown represent separate runs, not a requirement to run them concurrently. Engineering plan based on the project protocol and discussion. Codex CLI reference: https://developers.openai.com/codex/cli ."}